<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kassites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kassite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kassites were a people, probably from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> region, who took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-sack-of-babylon">Hittite sack of Babylon</a> and ruled it for about four centuries. They adopted Babylonian culture and the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>. They came into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>, which finally ended their rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the Kassites is uncertain. Several names thought to be Kassite appear in economic documents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> period, but their origin there is ambiguous, and many historians place their original homeland in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>. One theory makes them a Caucasian people akin to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a> with an Indo-European ruling element; another, disputed, derives them from the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. Their language is most likely an isolate, related to neither Indo-European nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, though some linguists propose a link to Hurro-Urartian. The earliest person with a Kassite name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> is recorded in 1770 BCE, and a year name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> refers to a campaign against the Kassite army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Hittite sack of Babylon in 1531 BCE the Kassites took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, which they held until about 1155 BCE on the short chronology. Their rulers regained the idol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, returned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and made him the equal of the Kassite god Shuqamuna; they renamed the city Karanduniash. The fall of the First Sealand dynasty in 1460 BCE left a power vacuum that they filled. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> in the early 14th century BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> rose, and the Kassites, Hittites and Assyrians formed a three-way balance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> exerting influence, joined by trade, treaties and intermarriage. Fourteen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna Letters</a> are exchanges between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> and the Kassite king.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurigalzu-i">Kurigalzu I</a>, in the mid-14th century BCE, the Kassites conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. He also founded the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dur-kurigalzu">Dur-Kurigalzu</a>, with palaces and temples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninlil">Ninlil</a> and Ninurta. Conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> continued for centuries, and for a time early in the reign of Tukulti-Ninurta I Assyria had the upper hand. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shutrukid-dynasty">Shutrukid dynasty</a> finally conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> in the 12th century BCE and carried off the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>. According to the Assyrian Synchronistic Chronicle, which is not considered reliable, the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil-nadin-ahi">Enlil-nadin-ahi</a>, was taken to Susa in 1155 BCE and died there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Kassite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> was ruled by kings through a bureaucracy supported by an aristocracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> warriors, and their arrival coincided with the introduction of the horse-drawn chariot. Over the centuries the Kassites were absorbed into the Babylonian population, and eight of the last kings bore Akkadian names. After the dynasty was overthrown the provincial administration continued, and the country remained united under the Second Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>. About 12,000 documents of the period have been recovered, only about a tenth of them published; kudurrus, stone stelae recording land grants, are another major source. Groups called Kassites, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Cossaei, lived in the mountains for centuries and were a target of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s last winter campaign, in 324/323 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kassites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Kassites</b>, who had pressed on Babylonia's borders for generations, moved in to establish a ruling dynasty that would govern the country for more than four centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It later belonged to the Sealand Dynasty, to the <b>Kassites</b> from the 16th century BCE, when Kurigalzu I rebuilt the temple of Ningal, at times to the Middle Assyrian Empire, and to the Neo-Assyrian Empire from the 10th to the late 7th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Kassites</b>, a people from the Zagros with their own language and gods, had settled in Mesopotamia in the 18th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kassite-babylonia">Kassite Babylonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Some rulers bore names suggesting links with the <b>Kassites</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hana">Kingdom of Hana</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Hittite sack of Babylon in 1595 BCE, the <b>Kassites</b> took the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/sealand-dynasty">Sealand dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
